#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133702 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c67da0d8-518b-5fdb-b6ba-6c1c95c22d9b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"}] :question/id #uuid "d6e8fac3-5dfc-5487-bd81-dc33aeae5f1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9097" :question/problem "![](images/83/83bd636f3a294c641e171c5a8281aaa7068e1931a06229064314e8f96efd0c3a.png)\n\nCalculate ![](images/c4/c4d83a5e5d029ef5f5e06b68d3cc39ea72ecf0c404f18d6a0ded750b0e2a6f39.svg) where ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) be the region bounded by ![](images/27/278f99df1e14fe0ebfb61bdb72262a62670dcaa60d322148bc3ee591b278ec2d.svg) If ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) and ![](images/4b/4be111afe7c23428ae5024a8bb9424de4d5b9e8cce74d2258fdffb9bdca9bcfd.svg) have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing ![](images/87/87e7e0743ecd6609730f4b9a9b3681b1f360ca86bcb209e03561a197b18782f3.svg) then Green's theorem states that\n![](images/ac/accf185e064f4c9c99cc6ad1437aabc7d5471cc332ff6c38d51a3f11ec6e26f2.svg)\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that ![](images/09/090c7fe781ec77412a904bb44dda28812c8a4e9a8524886e36041ffcabe9f0a4.svg) and ![](images/d7/d719ec6c4bb88290d50d81fc268cc438cb3002659b6ac79210889d779a09d44c.svg) So,\n\n![](images/94/94e9af3cff89c9bcf412776abcab67d1852ba083401829edcd746cb19311c8b9.svg)\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n![](images/ec/ec5ca2469ae1fd23385b27e906cb952f6b07c2bbfe02acbc42afa20284d4a122.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133715 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56caab23-f54d-58ee-99db-99c651f78e5c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Circulation of a Vector Field Over a Region Containing Holes"}] :question/id #uuid "18eef985-0fd4-5b08-8072-684ded227ab4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9557" :question/problem "![](images/16/16026d4ac7deeb30c1abb3a066df23a06a544116463b364b0a1e9e0bac0608c8.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = 10, \n\\qquad \n\\oint\\limits_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = -8, \n\\qquad \n\\oint\\limits_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = 3,\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ and $C_3$ are oriented clockwise, and $C_2$ is oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve $C_1$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_1$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves $C_2$ and $C_3$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_2$ and $C_3$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/11/11445c083440ee2fde1864d599a8bbd68146d522c61de11b3e90dc231e6eddf1.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the outer curve $C_1$ and the inner curve $C_2$ are opposite those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-10-(-8)+3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133716 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdd9b289-4285-5958-a9ef-853882a18d2b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes: Advanced Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "defe326f-f578-568c-a9b3-3d01f67da058" :question/math-academy-id "e-9558" :question/problem "![](images/64/64ba92b021de2ba2f4fc98f1ce76fa0d16b3c8b7ed9ae45237cb8772b111c8b8.png)\n\n  \n\nCalculate \n$\\displaystyle\n\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} (2x^3y^2-x^5) \\,\\mathrm{d}x + (3x^4y+y^4) \\,\\mathrm{d}y,$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x^3y^2-x^5$ and $Q(x,y)= 3x^4y+y^4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 4x^3y, \\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 12x^3y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-{x}^{5})\\,dx+(3{x}^{4}y+{y}^{4})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12{x}^{3}y-4{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as $\\Delta_1\\setminus\\Delta_2,$ where\n\n- $\\Delta_1 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 1, -1 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- $\\Delta_2 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq x \\leq 0, 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{|}_{-2}^{1}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-16)\\cdot (4-1)-(0-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =(-15)\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-44.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdd9b289-4285-5958-a9ef-853882a18d2b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4e662c6-8699-54e7-97af-a7c14d402002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130763} {:answer/id #uuid "f69c5e62-4eaa-562e-b551-1ab0def8e8e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "138" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130764} {:answer/id #uuid "09f9fc3a-138d-57c4-811a-5930d1b13580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130765} {:answer/id #uuid "3d68c829-31e9-571e-a524-13a9c5b60036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130766} {:answer/id #uuid "4a1ba910-c08a-502c-9cad-2f9758787120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "169" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130767}] :answer-field/id #uuid "af970c9a-4925-5b83-89f9-f1d57c4e3ec6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130762} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aeb67126-f2b3-5385-ae58-f6aa47dd1135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369394} {:answer/id #uuid "6a1f51d1-849c-5c40-98ed-fc855b6f7397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369395} {:answer/id #uuid "1cbc2426-d8bc-5d9e-b636-6dd81e32cf26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "138" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369396} {:answer/id #uuid "ee3e82f7-ed7f-5766-8b2b-b24890ff949c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369397} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba0ac0c-d2ca-5864-b0a8-1497f9993024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "169" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cbc2426-d8bc-5d9e-b636-6dd81e32cf26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "138" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369396} :answer-field/id #uuid "83851aef-4707-5c37-b41d-06a411ef4f5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "23de2113-f2a7-50b0-94a3-c0894b2b5ab4" :question/math-academy-id "q-126645" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6bda5a10df8a1ec081db97e7aa52755796f1d50be5447e3e64f55986116ada6e.png)\n\nCalculate \n${∮}_{∂D}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{y}^{2})-2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-4\\le x\\le 4,-3\\le y\\le 3}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,-1\\le y\\le 2}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =x\\,{|}_{-4}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-3}^{3}-x\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =(4-(-4))\\cdot (9-(-9))-(2-0)\\cdot (\\frac{8}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 18-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =138.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56caab23-f54d-58ee-99db-99c651f78e5c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Circulation of a Vector Field Over a Region Containing Holes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a658638-403e-5ab9-a8f1-33237b22ab86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497887} {:answer/id #uuid "4d5743a8-8fd6-5394-b293-eb9fd93fef49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497979} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9eb833-f8ff-5a68-9ca4-2b64e1730afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498127} {:answer/id #uuid "9d75ef58-226e-55fd-b151-11b12d2230bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498153} {:answer/id #uuid "f496543e-10b7-5658-8197-b5afed861eb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a658638-403e-5ab9-a8f1-33237b22ab86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497887} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7d01b58-0857-54fb-bdf6-61d33e711eeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2260d96-b984-5295-801c-28a9593008a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-126851" :question/problem "![](images/e9/e9892ef4efa3481ad02f7b8e38f1bae1876b196627204521d011656010297281.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=16,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=6,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=4,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/b2/b20b2b911c2a417c26fa951d9b28efa296c265df25e9d06b635ef4846267b93b.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =16+6+4 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c67da0d8-518b-5fdb-b6ba-6c1c95c22d9b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fe866fa-6339-5f10-8ce3-3d5938c3785e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369436} {:answer/id #uuid "0aafa5fa-6806-5c1a-bd6e-a8f0ec680d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369437} {:answer/id #uuid "b169f03c-00d2-5df3-887b-9ea86b4a1f34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369438} {:answer/id #uuid "2e03cd88-a4dd-5d49-b397-096aeb5b4219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369439} {:answer/id #uuid "8e91bdb9-02ae-5c29-aa67-a875b2bc2112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e03cd88-a4dd-5d49-b397-096aeb5b4219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369439} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2ee2d3e-bbe0-5737-8885-1738f355a09b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32a18b85-d978-5b18-8fff-914bdccf82dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-126853" :question/problem "![](images/39/393bed476f2e8d84fc7e5dc721d77749f7f259834e03a94a48a08533ae9befa6.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}2y\\,dx+4x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2y$ and $Q(x,y)=4x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}2y\\,dx+4x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (6\\cdot 4-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (24-4) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431351 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56caab23-f54d-58ee-99db-99c651f78e5c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Circulation of a Vector Field Over a Region Containing Holes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca980d7-830a-5712-906f-d7004a38953f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431285} {:answer/id #uuid "2da41db7-6d0c-5183-9ea5-02fe84e79cce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431292} {:answer/id #uuid "3b720f7a-85ac-5b6e-aa55-b3fe1bb64322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431298} {:answer/id #uuid "750e0059-fe60-559a-b87a-afcd74ddf718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431312} {:answer/id #uuid "8173bde3-749c-53ab-95a4-d623b09672f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ca980d7-830a-5712-906f-d7004a38953f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431285} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6feb552-f724-5d46-86df-32005b0ffcc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c72e07b-dd70-5ff7-8f10-0939e87dedc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126855" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f26ee97bb47430c6a5ed314af007f49415295f682eeb474af33217ef58999758.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=4,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=9,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-5,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/7b/7b68ab924946d0509b1666b5c0795a7f8987c32aef88ca3ab72746663e9428a8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =4+9+(-5) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56caab23-f54d-58ee-99db-99c651f78e5c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Circulation of a Vector Field Over a Region Containing Holes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d7cf9a0-e8a9-528c-9faf-b2259025de3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369464} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1af42a-4bf9-5730-a278-44c2bcb271c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369465} {:answer/id #uuid "26f52db4-19ad-52bc-bdea-a43ddccabe76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369466} {:answer/id #uuid "74b55cd4-e132-5c25-af68-783e5644f0be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369467} {:answer/id #uuid "5901a191-1792-51d7-92cf-8ae7fe658d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5901a191-1792-51d7-92cf-8ae7fe658d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369468} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0c368b0-4e71-50a8-b81a-cf6d68ab083b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca63e980-b1ab-5e66-b1ce-ccb3144f7558" :question/math-academy-id "q-126862" :question/problem "![](images/49/49b14da54c952d0585f7aa894d618838ddc91beb65216e0c18c014ba5ad268d7.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=9,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-4,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-6,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/a8/a8e93055fb9a460ab7757475c9c381c191204eae72231921da870b8d727bf988.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =9+(-4)+(-6) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c67da0d8-518b-5fdb-b6ba-6c1c95c22d9b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0662a46f-bec7-54ca-b240-f60d5d5f1afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518998} {:answer/id #uuid "0e19b2e0-2b38-57c0-b50d-3e99a4fb345c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519013} {:answer/id #uuid "12efe453-d9bc-5e34-bdeb-04e1a1d22aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519022} {:answer/id #uuid "a0fe6c9c-6868-551d-8a19-e70fb67ab8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519256} {:answer/id #uuid "d13ea829-20bc-5000-ad9a-b878fad5ad6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0662a46f-bec7-54ca-b240-f60d5d5f1afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518998} :answer-field/id #uuid "69041780-f401-5aab-a5e0-f58cc39dca02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "881df761-fca2-5afa-aea8-d6a6e63a7521" :question/math-academy-id "q-126866" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f1dd3c66e3bf7d25f286720b360d6af1d6361804ab3ff52835d94f78e5084ee.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-3y$ and $Q(x,y)=2x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-(-3))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =5\\cdot ({4}^{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (16-2) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56caab23-f54d-58ee-99db-99c651f78e5c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Circulation of a Vector Field Over a Region Containing Holes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a1d2c6b-2930-525d-8d87-b98d293ac862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369485} {:answer/id #uuid "b507d870-7a81-57fc-907a-7e0a5f10c87d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369486} {:answer/id #uuid "26edd21f-0fb1-5cee-a037-f2ac92849637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369487} {:answer/id #uuid "c5449ea3-86e0-567c-994b-5a2e62f052d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369488} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa50445-3382-50ec-b369-94e7f20cec60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26edd21f-0fb1-5cee-a037-f2ac92849637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369487} :answer-field/id #uuid "66710c87-9546-58da-b5cb-2d949ea2fa53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1e9162c-9f73-505c-8f10-aceab91f0c27" :question/math-academy-id "q-126867" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b2754f286de30f2017974030e571ec52f85bb84ad633912922a2b514728f3a7b.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=6,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-10,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented clockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/85/85a8ea287d2cb8e3b70e332ac444a9e06262fef35369d1bad1f24ea5c9d31687.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the outer and inner curves are opposite those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-6-2-(-10) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56caab23-f54d-58ee-99db-99c651f78e5c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Circulation of a Vector Field Over a Region Containing Holes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19b2f076-3c1b-5b41-b54d-acbf22c85f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369492} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf840d4-a3b7-5a1f-8ab9-000eaa11d729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369493} {:answer/id #uuid "544f331d-d37a-57d2-a8ec-3849a346d73c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369494} {:answer/id #uuid "7953c459-d75a-5d7a-a051-42cf152ae33e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369495} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7aedd6-9474-596f-83c6-b28c824b5114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7953c459-d75a-5d7a-a051-42cf152ae33e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369495} :answer-field/id #uuid "998a54d8-ef67-567f-aff6-05f6543bcc9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9bfcd0d-e2e4-5ddd-944e-eb308c18a16d" :question/math-academy-id "q-126870" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f308d1a3ecf3bd0090e22c4f70389311db4db3ff06f7038db0cf7770a8837c7.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=5,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-2,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-9,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are oriented clockwise and ${C}_{3}$ is oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/2b/2bed1b367cd101e0e90674c619ad87da8c90744df570eeea4d9b0333ccee0de1.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the outer curve ${C}_{1}$ and the inner curve ${C}_{3}$ are opposite those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-5+(-2)-(-9) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c67da0d8-518b-5fdb-b6ba-6c1c95c22d9b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68a479bb-7541-5424-a117-bc163a220ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441596} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb606cd-8859-52ad-923b-4174c43b9bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441599} {:answer/id #uuid "bab36efc-1119-58ba-bc4b-a7d41daed2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441602} {:answer/id #uuid "d102eb68-b278-588e-9ae4-718a08bc02d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441604} {:answer/id #uuid "fbd69b33-186c-5c7d-b495-4a5c8ed20698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d102eb68-b278-588e-9ae4-718a08bc02d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441604} :answer-field/id #uuid "25ffc2c7-e514-54e6-8c7b-600c1de04088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93b19a79-b263-5685-8630-5024a89d98bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-126893" :question/problem "![](images/df/df52e2add706a17bf93236cdaa04582932b6c799d4a88a3e194a3a3788c816ff.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}3y\\,dx+7x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3y$ and $Q(x,y)=7x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7-3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8\\pi  \\\\  & =32\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c67da0d8-518b-5fdb-b6ba-6c1c95c22d9b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e6750b8-75e8-5604-bdf3-5b26e689ff79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369499} {:answer/id #uuid "71c2554d-1b8f-50ee-904e-bf572ea954dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369500} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d1e293-45b2-553f-8738-81af539d167d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369501} {:answer/id #uuid "808c516e-8fb0-5d9d-b3c3-71bd5e0c0d18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369502} {:answer/id #uuid "56bd1130-5922-503b-9779-186620077dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3d1e293-45b2-553f-8738-81af539d167d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369501} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a4245ec-3053-53c4-bb04-e49df5e84b57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3c22304-91b2-51e6-94d5-71e3689e1379" :question/math-academy-id "q-126894" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e86380c457bc9d37d0180057d4ce8c035055986d32254d95fab3a69254531a5.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}6y\\,dx+9x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6y$ and $Q(x,y)=9x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(9-6)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 5\\pi  \\\\  & =15\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c67da0d8-518b-5fdb-b6ba-6c1c95c22d9b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb8d995d-0d7c-54d6-8c3f-aa2cc0e562a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498216} {:answer/id #uuid "ce93c3a1-776f-5631-aafd-89bd7f33c5b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498268} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe50e10-ec80-526e-8c44-4ebed28fb715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498270} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f112d4-518e-59d2-a8ae-9b7896ed80c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498304} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e01110-89b2-522a-b30e-7b877b8b16ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfe50e10-ec80-526e-8c44-4ebed28fb715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498270} :answer-field/id #uuid "30362ef7-7428-53da-a7f2-7fe7cc3d3d5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09c35a82-4469-5857-aed4-331c374b8ab3" :question/math-academy-id "q-126895" :question/problem "![](images/0d/0db447407835f74476edd20ed5327ff3cc570905f744bbc6c29a66926913ecb5.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}(1-4y)\\,dx+(2+5x)\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=1-4y$ and $Q(x,y)=2+5x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-(-4))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}9\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =9\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 5\\pi  \\\\  & =45\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdd9b289-4285-5958-a9ef-853882a18d2b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236daf0a-8fec-5986-adef-4fec2089883e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431289} {:answer/id #uuid "2980d347-4cd0-508a-a19e-609126e74ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431291} {:answer/id #uuid "b95f6d7e-5d5f-5174-809f-affea38f8fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "140\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431328} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d3bde0-ed52-52bf-9585-a17e40e4ed16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431329} {:answer/id #uuid "f2034a12-3dc0-57c4-b9a4-d7673fd61b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2034a12-3dc0-57c4-b9a4-d7673fd61b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431336} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b281059-95fd-5ff3-8dbf-e28f1dfbb9f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93158d64-29fd-580d-9bf5-f56c5e2adb43" :question/math-academy-id "q-126938" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5ca9f071eaeebb0b31fba99a48ca8f08bd6b4b73588838aad7b5f513c283fd6c.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3})\\,dx+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3})\\,dx+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot ({3}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =160\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdd9b289-4285-5958-a9ef-853882a18d2b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "247e7c9b-86e2-5f51-8ba9-c9448bc0dc8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431290} {:answer/id #uuid "408f7865-0008-58e6-85c6-ac75d60946d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "189" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431301} {:answer/id #uuid "4618a806-c076-56af-a407-64d1881a0e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431302} {:answer/id #uuid "51464593-b024-55b5-8ac6-2bf2b769ffdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431306} {:answer/id #uuid "c459028d-7c56-50dd-8fbf-e3321e5298d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "247e7c9b-86e2-5f51-8ba9-c9448bc0dc8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431290} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a97b187-3262-5c06-b7ed-fed9c7d6dc5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "420adaa9-fbd3-5686-bca6-a55b93dae680" :question/math-academy-id "q-126939" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b1a063fd261e790a8c3a56f741d0356f963300c3fbd88ddde03aa1744ef81053.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,dx+({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{3})\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{4}$ and $Q(x,y)=({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{3}).$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,dx+({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}-(-4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y+3{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}3\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot ((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{4}3{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{3{r}^{5}}{5}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))\\cdot (\\frac{3(4)^{5}}{5}-\\frac{3(1)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =(1-1)\\cdot (\\frac{3(4)^{5}}{5}-\\frac{3(1)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdd9b289-4285-5958-a9ef-853882a18d2b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006e746d-7483-59b3-ba9c-8cd280691f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369527} {:answer/id #uuid "64fc0003-b805-567e-8d14-5ab416216a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369528} {:answer/id #uuid "14561ae1-e3be-5f9d-bccd-fde3e73d6059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369529} {:answer/id #uuid "da7adf00-3485-5b42-ac23-7efeb71d22ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369530} {:answer/id #uuid "89cc8f37-3c67-5183-a3bc-6cf27168df13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89cc8f37-3c67-5183-a3bc-6cf27168df13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369531} :answer-field/id #uuid "1303c02b-a3ee-52e9-98a2-763dbec18e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "904872cd-e02c-55d9-9792-17de482b0907" :question/math-academy-id "q-126945" :question/problem "![](images/75/754aa1ded10b20362350f3da9e5b4dbe5f9f194c2289af348ff9de34f2fb8cc5.png)\n\nCalculate \n${∮}_{∂D}(x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\,dy,$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy-2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,-1\\le y\\le 2}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}2xy\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(4-4)\\cdot (2-\\frac{1}{2})-(1-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdd9b289-4285-5958-a9ef-853882a18d2b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12bc592f-463c-5b10-94de-5b44d176d00d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{89}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441590} {:answer/id #uuid "1e63af1d-9e8f-51ce-b89b-69668a98103f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441591} {:answer/id #uuid "6f81656c-ec2b-52fd-99b0-096af9bbcd54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441597} {:answer/id #uuid "e220a614-c017-51de-896b-5544add5e4e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441606} {:answer/id #uuid "fbafbae6-9a9a-5e89-b7aa-04db6e10b82c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbafbae6-9a9a-5e89-b7aa-04db6e10b82c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441608} :answer-field/id #uuid "25134893-42bb-5ae1-bdf7-77b092bdf96e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "311f57fc-3b6c-595d-a8b9-91e8d4d5d656" :question/math-academy-id "q-126947" :question/problem "![](images/82/823ab320e0f1426b846c7bf561b977ac6458c80d235aabdc5647a7db07c4cb74.png)\n\nCalculate \n${∮}_{∂D}(2x{y}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,dx+(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,dy,$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x{y}^{3}+{x}^{5}$ and $Q(x,y)=5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=10x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(2x{y}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,dx+(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}10x{y}^{2}-6x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 2,-2\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}4x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-2}^{1}-2{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =(8-2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}-(-\\frac{8}{3}))-(2-0)\\cdot (0-(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 3-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =18-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
